    "agent_last_message": "The tool is finished: `output/main.py` is a single file that uses only the standard library, and its 39 tests pass. It handles every edge case in the spec, and on a 279 MB file its line, word and character counts match `wc` exactly.\n\n```\ncd output\npython main.py --input notes.txt --output report.json\npython -m unittest discover -s tests     # run the tests\n```\n\n**How it works.** It reads the file in 64 KiB chunks and counts words on the raw bytes, then decodes and lowercases each distinct word once. That gets the spec's rules right in two places where Python's normal string handling would not:\n- Only the six whitespace characters the spec lists separate words. Python's `str.split()` would also split on 23 others, such as the no-break space.\n- Characters are counted as bytes, so `caf\u00e9` counts as 5.\n\n**Testing**\n- The chunked reading is checked against a separate, simpler implementation on random inputs, with chunks as small as 1 byte.\n- I broke the code on purpose in 9 likely ways, and each one made the tests fail.\n- A final review found one real bug: invalid UTF-8 read from a named pipe would hang forever. It's fixed and has a test.\n\n**Speed**\n- A 280 MB file takes about 4.5 seconds (60\u201365 MB/s) and uses 27 MB of memory.\n- A small file takes about 35 ms, almost all of it Python starting up.\n- It uses one CPU core, so for a large collection run several copies at once (e.g. with `xargs -P`).\n- Memory grows with the number of distinct words: a file of 5 million different words used about 860 MB and ran at about 14 MB/s.\n\n**Decisions the spec left open**\n- **Text that isn't UTF-8 is treated as \"malformed\" and exits with 1.** The error message gives the position of the first bad byte, and no output file is written. This is the main judgment call: older Latin-1 or Windows-1252 files will be rejected. If you'd rather have them counted anyway, that's a small change.\n- **Lowercasing follows Unicode rules.** `\u00c9T\u00c9` and `\u00e9t\u00e9` count as the same word; `\u00df` stays `\u00df`.\n- **Exit code",
